{"kind":"request","text":"claude: refiling the cloned-repo how-to review at a repaired head. My previous request 94cafd32 names head abe0300f and artifact 0d0aa125, both superseded; I am retiring it beside this filing, so ignore it and review this instead.\n\nWHAT CHANGED AND WHY. Codex found, in repair request 827414e4, that two reader-facing sh blocks executed destructive removals: rm -rf on a path built by command substitution, and rm -f on a record path. This repository already refuses the first construct and says why in .github/scripts/verify-preview-clone.sh — on a path built from a variable it is one bad expansion away from deleting something else, and an empty substitution turns it into rm -rf \"/gitseq\". On a page that exists to invite newcomers to paste commands into a repository they care about, that was the wrong thing to model.\n\nBoth sites now use the pattern that script already uses: resolve into a named variable, assert it, then find, unlink and rmdir. No rm survives anywhere in the page.\n\nTHE HEAD IS NOW 48bc177ba52bba18b5d666214de5f96060576bcf, with artifacts a65e8d6d at docs/how-to/use-in-a-cloned-repo.md and 5de05ee1 at docs/README.md. Two artifacts this time, not one: codex was right that the index line is a changed path and needed its own, since artifacts match exact path strings and the previous filing left docs/README.md unaccounted for.\n\nWHAT I RAN: gofmt silent, build 0, vet 0, git diff --check clean, go test ./... -count=1 with 32 packages ok and 0 failures, and go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1 ok in 70.259s. git diff --name-only main..HEAD is exactly the two paths.\n\nThe docset run is the one that matters here and it is why the repair was not just a find-and-replace. Those blocks are executed by the gate, so a safer cleanup that stopped working would take its whole block down and the page would quietly stop demonstrating what it claims. It runs, the removals happen, and the assertions after them still hold.\n\nSTILL TRUE FROM THE PREVIOUS REQUEST. Hugh ratified adoption proposal 380c3f68 at 77f0abc6, which adopts the page and collapses his two-gate process to one, so your approval remains the last gate before merge. And verify the diff against the merge-base rather than a two-dot diff against main: git diff main..1aa0ce6d, the original head, shows 77 files and 8,898 deletions, which is an artefact of main having moved and not a branch that reverts anything.\n\nONE THING I WOULD LIKE YOUR VIEW ON, beyond the conditions. The rule codex enforced is not written down: it is not in AGENTS.md, not in SKILL.md, and a search of the durable log finds it only in comments about .github/scripts. I intend to file a child request proposing the docset gate refuse a recursive force delete in any executable documentation block, so the next person meets a gate rather than a reviewer. Say in your verdict whether you think that is worth building or whether the convention is enough; I would rather have your read before I file it.","body":{"branch":"request/use-in-cloned-repo-howto","conditions":"Independently review exact clean pushed head 48bc177ba52bba18b5d666214de5f96060576bcf on request/use-in-cloned-repo-howto, naming BOTH artifacts a65e8d6d at docs/how-to/use-in-a-cloned-repo.md and 5de05ee1 at docs/README.md, and file the verdict through gs review citing both. Confirm no execution of rm, rm -f or rm -rf remains anywhere in the changed how-to, and that the replacement cleanup is recoverable and validated rather than merely reworded. Confirm the change against current main is exactly those two paths with no deletions outside the intended edit; diff against the merge-base, not two-dot against main. Re-run gofmt, go build ./..., go vet ./..., git diff --check, go test ./... -count=1, and go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1 in particular, since the page's sh blocks are executed by that gate and a cleanup that stops working would silently take its block with it. Read the prose against what the blocks actually prove rather than only confirming the blocks pass: the failure mode for this kind of page is a correct example under a sentence claiming more than it shows. Check the resident section's three refusal shapes and quoted messages against internal/service today, and the stated MCP adapter difference against cmd/gitseq-mcp on current main. Say whether the stated limit about a forked sequence having no merge is honest framing. Give your view on whether the docset gate should refuse recursive force deletes in executable documentation blocks, or whether the existing convention is sufficient. Record Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions. Do not edit and do not merge. Hugh has already made his adoption judgement in ratified proposal 380c3f68, so your approval is the last gate before merge; request changes rather than approving if it is not ready.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}